  "body": "Found by the read-only second review of PR #2940. The finding comes from a read of the code. No run proves it.\n\n## What can happen\n\nPR #2940 exempts a loop from the partly-bevelled refusal of #2887 when the top section is the wall section moved rigidly (`leans` in `crates/motion/src/machining2d/chamfer_ride.rs`). That is correct for a wall that leans in a tilted frame.\n\nOne upright part meets the same test: a plate with a 1 x 45 degree chamfer on the -X side and a 45 degree undercut lip on the +X side that stands out exactly 1 mm. The two sections are `[-29,31] x [-20,20]` and `[-30,30] x [-20,20]`. They are exact translates, so `leans` is true. The chamfer is then cut all round. It breaks the sharp Y edges and the lip edge. That is the defect of #2887.\n\nThe case needs the lip offset to be equal to the chamfer offset within 1e-4 mm. It is not probable, but the result is a cut that the user did not ask for.\n\n## Possible repair\n\nPermit the exemption only when the shift is equal to the shift that the tilt of the operation frame gives. In an upright frame that shift is zero, and this part is refused again.\n\n## Done when\n\n- A test with this part pins the refusal.\n- The box under a 10 degree tilt (#2936) still generates.\n- The sentence \"Thus the new test cannot hide a case of #2887\" in `docs/decisions/2936-chamfer-leaning-wall.md` is corrected.\n\nRefs #2936, #2887\n",
